        id: "u9z2rr",
        title: "Variants in the original publication of “Lilies that Fester”",
        printedIn: "<i>Twentieth Century</i>",
        issueOrVolume: "vol. 157, no. 938 (April)",
        pageRange: "332-334",
        year: 1955,
        monthAndDay: null,
        textProvided: "[The following records where the original <i>Twentieth Century</i> (1955) text differs from the revised text reprinted in <i>They Asked for a Paper</i> (1962): the 1955 reading is given first, with the 1962 reading in square brackets.]<br><br>In your ‘Cambridge Number’ Mr John Allen asks (p. 127) [In the ‘Cambridge Number’ of the <i>Twentieth Century</i> (1955) Mr. John Allen asked]<br><br>Writing in these pages, I naturally produce my next parallel [I produce my next parallel]<br><br>well-bred or holy. But this need not be so. Such people [well-bred or holy. Such people]<br><br>But others think (I believe, rightly) that such talk [But there are others who sincerely and (I believe) rightly think that such talk]<br><br>I intend no <i>petitio</i> of the vexed question [I do not mean to beg the vexed question]<br><br>In <i>Howard’s End</i> Mr Forster [In <i>Howards End</i> Mr. E. M. Forster]<br><br>which, it is hoped (sometimes in vain), will arise [which, they think, will arise]<br><br>About <i>Culture</i> as the sensitive and enriching social life I have the same trouble. I was at first puzzled by Mr Forster’s doctrine (p. 100) that it is the duty of the individual ‘to create … other individuals’. All the people I had ever met (children and illiterates included) were individuals already. So I supposed<sup>1</sup> Mr Forster must mean that everyone was to assist and encourage everyone else in becoming even more individual than he was before. Now I gladly admit that there is a kind of behaviour (no commoner, in my experience, among the <i>cultured</i> than anywhere else) which does in fact liberate and enrich, instead of cramping and devouring, the personality of others. But I believe it is seldom inspired by anything so ambitious as an intention to ‘create’ the others or even to do them good. Indeed a desire to improve one’s neighbours most often leads to those very types of conduct which are most suffocating and encroaching. I think those who really liberate and enrich other personalities do so by qualities of a very humdrum and old-fashioned sort; some kinds (not, alas, all) of affection, a feeling for fair play, humility, respect for men, a sense of humour and of proportion. And I cannot conceive how any of these could survive the sense of a ‘duty to create other individuals’. For the very idea of approaching one’s fellows with such creative intentions savours of something very like impertinence and thus parts company at the very outset with that respect, that regard for his inviolability and independence, which is the first and most indispensable condition of our producing the effect Mr Forster has in view. Perhaps the man does not want to be, in your sense, ‘created’; or not by you. If you avowed your purpose (and could the most brazen of ‘humanists’ do it without embarrassment?) he might tell you to mind your own business. To be sure, if my neighbour has adopted Mr Forster’s ethical system, he will be simultaneously trying to create me; but that would surely be as likely to lead to deadlock or head-on collision as to any valuable result. I wish we had Molière back; he would have written a fine dialogue between two self-commissioned, reciprocal creators. [In <i>They Asked for a Paper</i> (1962) this paragraph is replaced by the passage beginning “So much for the individual. But the claims made for the ‘cultured’ group raise an embarrassing question …”]<br><br>To all these manifestations of <i>Culture</i> my objection is the same. They use, [This would be to use consciously and self-consciously, as means to extraneous ends, things which must lose all their power of conducing to those ends by the very fact of being so used.]<br><br><sup>1</sup> But not before I had consulted all the readers of Mr Forster’s letter whom I could find. Three other interpretations of his sentence were canvassed: (<i>a</i>) That we ought all to have as many children as we possibly can. But this seems unplausible, and anyway Mr Forster is too good a writer to use such a big word as <i>create</i> if he only meant <i>beget</i> or <i>have</i>. (<i>b</i>) That we ought all to become novelists or playwrights and create imaginary characters. But since Mr Forster is prescribing duties for ‘the individual’ (which must, in ordinary English, mean ‘all individuals’), this would be absurd. For he knows that many of us can’t create in that sense, and a duty must be something possible. (<i>c</i>) That we must educate people in such a way that they become more individual. That since the duty is laid upon all individuals, this would mean we must all so educate one another. And that comes to nearly the same thing as the interpretation I have adopted in the text.<br><br>summed up in Mr Allen’s phrase, ‘the faith in culture’ [summed up in Mr. Allen’s phrase in the <i>Twentieth Century</i> ‘the faith in culture’]<br><br>Now a step further. Mr Allen complains that [Now a step further. Mr. Allen complained that]<br><br>There are at this point (still p. 127) a good many allusions [There are at this point a good many allusions]<br><br>which he borrows from a Papist [which he borrows from a Christian]<br><br>how I think it would come about [how I think it could come about]<br><br>Notice that Mr Allen speaks of rebels [Notice that Mr. Allen spoke of rebels]<br><br>would ‘create an individual’; helpless plasticine [would create a character; helpless Plasticine]<br><br>anticipation of responses, and a method inappropriate [anticipation of responses, and of a method inappropriate]<br><br>in some degree of other [in some degree or other]<br><br>entitled ‘Poetry in the Managerial Age’. He there [entitled ‘Poetry in the Managerial Age’ (<i>Essays in Criticism</i>, iv, 3, July 1954). He there]<br><br>Mr Allen falls short of the mark when he speaks of [Mr. Allen fell short of the mark when he spoke of]<br><br>the question discussed in this article has nothing [the question has nothing]<br><br>It is for them I have written. And to them I say [It is for them I have written. To them I say]<br><br>[The 1962 reprint also makes numerous purely typographical and house-style changes not recorded here — single quotation marks changed to double, “Mr.” given a full stop, British spellings (<i>practises</i>→<i>practices</i>, <i>organizes</i>→<i>organises</i>, <i>hypostatized</i>→<i>hypostatised</i>), em-dash spacing, and minor punctuation.]",
        type: "Prose",
        notes: ["This passage was rewritten when “Lilies that Fester” was republished in <i>They Asked for a Paper</i> (London: Geoffrey Bles, 1962), 108-110."]
