        year: 2019,
        monthAndDay: null,
        textProvided: "[{108} From ACRP/30, about Coverdale:]<br><br>We were told to keep his words where possible<br><br>[{108} From ACRP/30. In one specific instance, Lewis writes on Psalm 2:8:] <br><br>...we can keep Coverdale; therefore we should<br><br>[{108} Source unclear but probably ACRP/30. On a verse in Psalm 10, Lewis considers the change from <i>wherefore</i> to <i>why</i> to be]<br><br>a wholly gratuitous departure from C.<br><br>[{108} Source unclear but probably ACRP/30. In his written comments on Psalms 19, 20, and 21, Lewis writes about]<br><br>a needless change of C.<br><br>let’s keep C.<br><br>No difference in sense, so let’s keep C.<br><br>[{110} Source unclear. In one note on Psalm 15:5 (verse 4 in the English) Lewis writes about promises:]<br><br>In plain masculine Modern English the only two things you do with a promise are to <i>keep</i> it or <i>break</i> it. (<i>Implementing</i> be damned!).<br><br>[{112} Source unclear. In their discussion of Psalm 102, Lewis comments:]<br><br>In [Coverdale’s] time <i>secure</i> usually meant <i>securus,</i> not ‘safe’ but ‘feeling safe’. cf. <i>Macbeth</i> ‘security is mortals’ greatest enemy.’ <br><br>[{112} Source unclear. Lewis argues that they ought not to state in Psalm 11:3 that the foundations are cast down:]<br><br>We can’t have what is already the lowest (foundations) cast down: it’s like talking about falling off the floor.<br><br>[{112} Source unclear. Lewis comments playfully on the translation of Psalm 104:26:]<br><br>Suggest ‘to take thy pastime with them’ (If the Hebrew is equally tolerant of either, don’t let’s miss this delightful picture. Quite topical, now that we’re teaching dolphins to talk!).<br><br>[Finally, Heck provides two tables comparing the 1928 Psalter with Lewis’s suggested revision and the actual text of the Revised Psalter. What follows is just the second column of these tables, i.e. Lewis’s revisions. In what follows the page number and then the verse of the Psalm is given for each row in the column:]<br><br>[{119} 12:2a] They speak falsely everyone to his neighbour:<br>[{119} 12:6] I will up, saith the Lord, and defend him: I will give him peace as his heart desireth.<br>[{119} 12:8] O Lord preserve and save us: from a corrupt and evil generation.<br>[{119} 12:9b] : wickedness is exalted among the children of men.<br>[{119} 26:1b] : my trust also hath been steadfast in the Lord.<br>[{119} 26:2b] : try out my inward parts.<br>[{119} 26:4] I have not dwelt with sinners: neither have I fellowship with the hypocrites.<br>[{119} 26:9] O let me not be numbered with the transgressors: neither accounted as one of the blood-thirsty.<br>[{119} 26:10b] : and their right hand is full of bribes.<br>[{120} 23:3a] He shall renew my soul:<br>[{120}23:5a] Thou shalt prepare a table before me in the sight of them that trouble me:<br>[{120} 41:2] The Lord preserveth his life and maketh him mighty in the land: neither suffereth he his enemies to eat him up.<br>[{120} 41:3] The Lord comforteth him when he lieth sick: he maketh all his bed in his sickness.<br>[{120} 41:5] Mine enemies account me as one that is at the gates of death: they ask, When shall he die and his name perish?<br>[{120} 41:6] If they come to see me, they dissemble with their talk: of very malice they foretell the things that I fear, and when they go from me they spread the same abroad.<br>[{121} 41:7b] : they prophesy that my end is near.<br>[{121} 41:8] They say, this sickness is unto death: now that he lieth, he shall rise up no more.<br>[{121} 41:11] Then shall I know that thou dost delight in me: and that mine enemy shall not triumph against me.<br>[{121} 41:12] Because of mine innocency thou upholdest me.",
